WEBVTT

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:03.060
>> Hurricane Florence came

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:07.585
by while I was working on StatQuest.

00:00:07.585 --> 00:00:10.820
Dark clouds filled the sky,

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:14.205
but that didn't stop StatQuest.

00:00:14.205 --> 00:00:19.870
StatQuest. Hello/ I'm Josh Starmer

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:21.395
and welcome to StatQuest.

00:00:21.395 --> 00:00:22.830
Today we're going to be talking about

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:24.745
some machine learning fundamentals,

00:00:24.745 --> 00:00:26.450
bias, and variance

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:28.950
and they're going to be clearly explained.

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:32.030
Imagine we measured the weight and height of

00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:33.230
a bunch of mice and

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:35.470
plotted the data on a graph.

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:37.630
Light mice tend to be

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:42.130
short and heavier mice tend to be taller.

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:44.330
But after a certain weight,

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:47.690
mice don't get any taller, just more obese.

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:50.170
Given this data, we would like to

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:52.830
predict mouse height given its weight.

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:54.770
For example, if you

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:57.250
told me your mouse weighed this much,

00:00:57.250 --> 00:00:59.210
then we might predict that

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:01.320
the mouse is this tall.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:03.470
Ideally, we would know

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:05.610
the exact mathematical formula that

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:06.990
describes the relationship

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:08.790
between weight and height.

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:10.770
But in this case,

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.330
we don't know the formula,

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:13.410
so we're going to use

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:14.850
two machine learning methods

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:17.330
to approximate this relationship.

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:19.450
However, I'll leave

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:21.090
the true relationship curve

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:23.190
in the figure for reference.

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:25.150
The first thing we do is

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:26.930
split the data into two sets,

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:27.830
one for training

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:29.330
the machine learning algorithms

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:31.200
and one for testing them.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.290
The blue dots are the training set

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:37.525
and the green dots are the testing set.

00:01:37.525 --> 00:01:40.400
Here is just the training set.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.030
The first machine learning algorithm that

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:45.260
we will use is linear regression,

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:48.030
AKA, least squares.

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:50.250
Linear regression fits

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:52.790
a straight line to the training set.

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:55.190
Note. The straight line

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:56.610
doesn't have the flexibility

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:58.650
to accurately replicate the arc

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:00.790
in the true relationship.

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:02.990
No matter how we try to fit

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:05.830
the line, it will never curve.

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:09.630
Thus, the straight line will never

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:11.270
capture the true relationship

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:12.510
between weight and height,

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:13.690
no matter how well we

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:15.730
fit it to the training set.

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:17.770
The inability for a

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:19.230
machine learning method like

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:21.250
linear regression to capture

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:24.450
the true relationship is called bias.

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:26.810
Because the straight line can't

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:29.150
be curved like the true relationship,

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:32.600
it has a relatively large amount of bias.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:35.350
Another machine learning method might

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:38.160
fit a squiggly line to the training set.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:41.110
The squiggly line is super flexible and

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:42.690
hugs the training set along

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:44.950
the arc of the true relationship.

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:47.510
Because the squiggly line can handle

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:49.250
the arc in the true relationship

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:50.510
between weight and height,

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:52.870
it has very little bias.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:56.150
We can compare how well the straight line and

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:57.570
the squiggly line fit

00:02:57.570 --> 00:02:58.790
the training set by

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:01.230
calculating their sums of squares.

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:03.570
In other words, we measure

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:06.250
the distances from the fit lines to the data,

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:09.070
square them, and add them up.

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:11.590
They are squared so that

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:13.130
negative distances do not

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:15.890
cancel out positive distances.

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:18.250
Notice how the squiggly line

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:19.930
fits the data so well

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:21.490
that the distances between

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:24.550
the line and the data are all zero.

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:26.650
In the contest to see

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:27.850
whether the straight line

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:29.130
fits the training set

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:30.950
better than the squiggly line,

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:33.645
the squiggly line wins.

00:03:33.645 --> 00:03:36.920
But remember, so far we've only

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:38.100
calculated the sums of

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:40.300
squares for the training set.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:43.100
We also have a testing set.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:45.340
Now let's calculate the sums

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:47.780
of squares for the testing set.

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:49.480
In the contest to

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:50.800
see whether the straight line

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:52.100
fits the testing set

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.140
better than the squiggly line,

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:57.100
the straight line wins.

00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:59.740
Even though the squiggly line did

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:02.300
a great job fitting the training set,

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:04.260
it did a terrible job

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:06.100
fitting the testing set.

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.360
In machine learning lingo,

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:10.140
the difference in fits between

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:12.800
datasets is called variance.

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.780
The squiggly line has low bias,

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:17.860
since it is flexible and can adapt to

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:19.280
the curve in the relationship

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:21.140
between weight and height.

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:23.120
But the squiggly line has

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:25.000
high variability because it

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:26.880
results in vastly different sums

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:29.560
of squares for different datasets.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:32.920
In other words, it's hard to predict how well

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:34.460
the squiggly line will perform

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:35.980
with future datasets.

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:37.990
It might do well sometimes

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:41.025
and other times it might do terribly.

00:04:41.025 --> 00:04:43.990
In contrast, the straight line

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:45.810
has relatively high bias,

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:47.330
since it cannot capture

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:48.750
the curve in the relationship

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:50.490
between weight and height.

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:52.110
But the straight line

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:54.030
has relatively low variance

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:55.750
because the sums of squares are

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:58.550
very similar for different datasets.

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:01.810
In other words, the straight line might only

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:03.270
give good predictions and

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:04.850
not great predictions,

00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:06.570
but they will be consistently

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:08.020
good predictions.

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:11.315
Bam. Oh, no.

00:05:11.315 --> 00:05:13.280
Terminology alert.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:14.990
Because the squiggly line

00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:16.890
fits the training set really well,

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:18.550
but not the testing set,

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:22.050
we say that the squiggly line is overfit.

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:25.590
In machine learning, the ideal algorithm has

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:26.990
low bias and can

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:29.900
accurately model the true relationship.

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:32.670
And it has low variability by

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:34.530
producing consistent predictions

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:36.830
across different datasets.

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:38.830
This is done by finding

00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:40.130
the sweet spot between

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:44.010
a simple model and a complex model.

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:47.680
Oh, no. Another terminology alert.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:49.680
Three commonly used methods

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:51.560
for finding the sweet spot between

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:53.460
simple and complicated models

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:55.710
are regularization,

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:58.300
boosting, and bagging.

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:01.220
The StatQuest on a random forest show

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:03.485
an example of bagging in action.

00:06:03.485 --> 00:06:06.020
And we'll talk about regularization

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:08.935
and boosting in future StatQuests.

00:06:08.935 --> 00:06:13.260
Double bam. Hooray. We've made

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:15.875
it to the end of another exciting StatQuest.

00:06:15.875 --> 00:06:17.400
If you like this StatQuest and

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:19.140
want to see more, please subscribe.

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:21.760
And if you want to support StatQuest,

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:23.400
please consider buying one

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:25.200
or two of my original songs.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:29.020
All right. Until next time, quest on.

